posts/c&c++/c++2.0/17 declval.html.body:
std::declval is a helper utility in C++ that converts any type T to its rvalue reference type (i.e. T&&). 
The usage of std::declval is commonly seen in the context of decltype for determining the type returned by a function or operation involving multiple argument types which are not actually instantiated.
for example:
#include 

template 
auto TestFunction(T1 val1, T2 val2) -> decltype(std::declval() + std::declval()) {
    return val1 + val2;
}

if we defined TestFunction as follows 
template   
auto TestFunction(T1 val1, T2 val2) -> decltype( T1() + T2()) { 
    return val1 + val2; 
}

The version attempts to default-construct instances of the types T1 and T2, and then add them together to deduce the return type using decltype. If T1 or T2 aren't default constructable,  this will be a problem.
Since std::declval is intended primarily for use in decltype expressions where it's never actually invoked, it doesn't need to (and doesn't) have a definition.  std::declval is declared in the  header of the C++ Standard Library. It's declaration might looks like this 
namespace std {
    template
    typename add_rvalue_reference::type declval() noexcept;
}

So std::declval can only be used in  unevaluated expressions, typically with decltype, or noexcept()
其实最简单的，
template   
auto TestFunction(T1 val1, T2 val2) -> decltype( val1 + val2 ) { 
    return val1 + val2; 
}

或者干脆省去(c++14)
template   
auto TestFunction(T1 val1, T2 val2) { 
    return val1 + val2; 
}

主要就是以后看到std::declval知道是个什么东西就行了
