A constructor cannot possibly recover and do something sensible after one of its base or member subobjects' constructors throws. It cannot even put its own object into a "construction failed" state recognizable to the compiler. Its object is not constructed; it never will be constructed no matter what Frankensteinian efforts the handler attempts in order to breathe life into the nonobject. And whatever destruction can be done has already been done automatically by the language; that includes all base and member subobjects.

What if your class can honestly have a sensible "construction partially failed" state—that is, it really does have some "optional" members that aren't strictly required and the object can limp along without them, possibly with reduced functionality? Then use the Pimpl idiom  to hold the possibly-bad parts of the object at arm's length. 

Incidentally, this also means that the only (repeat only) possible use for a constructor function try block is to translate an exception thrown from a base or member subobject.
What if i want to free resources allocated in the initializer list or in the constructor body!”

