posts/c&c++/其他/access specifiers.html.body:
When a class is declared as a base class in C++, it can have one of three access modifiers: public, protected, and private. These access specifiers control the visibility of members in the base class for the derived class and for the code that uses the derived class.
class BaseClass {
public:
    int publicMember;
protected:
    int protectedMember;
private:
    int privateMember;
};

class DerivedClassPublic : public BaseClass {
    // publicMember is accessible as public
    // protectedMember is accessible as protected
    // privateMember is not accessible
};

class DerivedClassProtected : protected BaseClass {
    // publicMember is accessible as protected
    // protectedMember is accessible as protected
    // privateMember is not accessible
};

class DerivedClassPrivate : private BaseClass {
    // publicMember is accessible as private
    // protectedMember is accessible as private
    // privateMember is not accessible
};

public: If a base class is inherited publicly, then the public members of the base class act as public members in the derived class and protected members of the base class act as protected members of the derived class.
protected: If a base class is inherited protectedly, then both the public and protected members of the base class act as protected members of the derived class.
private: If a base class is inherited privately, then both the public and protected members of the base class act as private members of the derived class.
In all of these cases, the private members of the base class are not accessible directly from the derived class, although they are still part of the derived object. If base class functions that access these private members are accessible in the derived class, those functions can be used to indirectly access or modify the private members in the context of a derived object.
