posts/c&c++/其他/contextual conversion to.body:
如下，我们如果给某个类的转换函数operator bool加上explicit
那么通常情况下，其转换为bool类型的操作都需要显示转换，但我们却可以在if语句中隐式的转换
class T {
public:
    explicit operator bool() const noexcept {
       // ...
       return true; 
    }
};

int main() {
    T t;
    if(t) { // This is fine!
        // Do something
    }

    bool b = t; // This will cause a compilation error due to "explicit"
    return 0;
}

The explicit keyword in C++ is used to prevent implicit conversions. When you declare a conversion operator with the explicit keyword, it means that the conversion can only happen explicitly, using a cast or direct initialization.
However, there is an exception when it comes to using objects in conditions, such as in if statements or while loops. In these contexts, the language allows a special kind of conversion known as “contextual conversion to bool.” This conversion is allowed even for types with an explicit conversion operator to bool.
The reason for this exception is to allow objects to be easily used in conditions where a boolean value is expected, without requiring an explicit cast. This is a convenience feature of the language that makes code more readable and easier to write.
So, when you have a type with an explicit operator bool(), you can still use objects of that type in conditions like if (t) without an explicit cast. The compiler will perform a contextual conversion to bool in this case, even though the conversion operator is explicit.
